{:edb/db-before-t 2105 :edb/db-after-t 2106 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535418 50 #inst "2026-10-08T23:43:44.884Z" 13194139535418 true] [17592186147313 1100 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t$. Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing damped harmonic motion?\n\n1. $y''+4y' - y = 0$\n2. $y''+ 4 = 0$\n3. $y''+y'+4y = 0$" 13194139535418 false] [17592186147313 1100 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing damped harmonic motion?\n\n- $y''+4y' - y = 0$\n\n- $y''+ 4 = 0$\n\n- $y''+y'+4y = 0$" 13194139535418 true] [17592186147313 1145 "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\ny'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- Equation I does not take the form\n$y'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = 0$.\nIt would require $\\omega^2=-1$, which is not possible for any real number $\\omega$ since $\\omega^2 \\geq 0$.\n- Equation II does not take the form\n$y'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = 0$\nbecause it has a constant term, $4$.\n- Equation III takes the form\n$y'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = 0$\nwith\n$\\omega^2 = 4 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\omega = 2$\nand $b = 1$. Therefore, equation III represents damped harmonic motion.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535418 false] [17592186147313 1145 "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\ny'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = 0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- Equation I does not take the form\n\n$$\ny'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = 0.\n$$\n\nIt would require $\\omega^2=-1,$ which is not possible for any real number $\\omega$ since $\\omega^2 \\geq 0.$\n\n- Equation II does not take the form\n\n$$\ny'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nbecause it has a constant term, $4.$\n\n- Equation III takes the form\n\n$$\ny'' + b y' + \\omega^2 y = 0\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\omega^2 = 4 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\omega = 2\n$$\n\nand $b = 1.$ Therefore, equation III represents damped harmonic motion.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535418 true] [17592186147314 1100 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $y'' +y'+2 y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping.\n\n- $y'' +2y'+ y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping.\n\n- $y'' +4y'+3y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping" 13194139535418 true] [17592186147314 1100 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y'' +y'+2 y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping.\n2. $y'' +2y'+ y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping.\n3. $y'' +4y'+3y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping" 13194139535418 false] [17592186147314 1145 "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $\\mathcal{D} > 0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $\\mathcal{D} = 0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $\\mathcal{D} < 0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation $y'' +y'+2 y = 0$ represents damped harmonic motion, and its characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 + \\lambda + 2 = 0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of the characteristic equation is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={1}^{2}-4(1)(2) & =-7<0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to light damping. Therefore, statement I is correct.\n\n- The differential equation $y'' +2y'+y = 0$ represents damped harmonic motion, and its characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 +2\\lambda + 1 = 0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of the characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={2}^{2}-4(1)(1) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to critical damping. Therefore, statement II is correct.\n\n- The differential equation $y'' +4y'+3 y$ represents damped harmonic motion, and its characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 +4\\lambda + 3 = 0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of the characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={4}^{2}-4(1)(3) & =4>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to heavy damping. Therefore, statement III is correct.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139535418 true] [17592186147314 1145 "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $\\mathcal{D} > 0$ leads to heavy damping,\n- $\\mathcal{D} = 0$ leads to critical damping, and\n- $\\mathcal{D} < 0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation $y'' +y'+2 y = 0$ represents damped harmonic motion, and its characteristic equation is\n$\\lambda^2 + \\lambda + 2 = 0$.\nThe discriminant of the characteristic equation is:\n$D = 1^{2} - 4(1)(2)|=-7 < 0$,\nwhich corresponds to light damping. Therefore, statement I is correct.\n- The differential equation $y'' +2y'+y = 0$ represents damped harmonic motion, and its characteristic equation is\n$\\lambda^2 +2\\lambda + 1 = 0$.\nThe discriminant of the characteristic equation is\n$D = 2^{2} - 4(1)(1)|= 0$,\nwhich corresponds to critical damping. Therefore, statement II is correct.\n- The differential equation $y'' +4y'+3 y$ represents damped harmonic motion, and its characteristic equation is\n$\\lambda^2 +4\\lambda + 3 = 0$.\nThe discriminant of the characteristic equation is\n$D = 4^{2} - 4(1)(3)|= 4 > 0$,\nwhich corresponds to heavy damping. Therefore, statement III is correct.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139535418 false] [17592186147315 1100 "A particle of mass $5\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 15\\, \\textrm{kg/s}^2$. The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity, and has a damping coefficient $c = 10\\, \\textrm{kg/s}$. The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" 13194139535418 false] [17592186147315 1100 "A particle of mass $5\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 15\\, \\textrm{kg/s}^2.$  The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity, and has a damping coefficient $c = 10\\, \\textrm{kg/s}.$  The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" 13194139535418 true] [17592186147315 1145 "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\nm y'' =-cy' -k y\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m$, $k$, and $c$ gives\n\n$$\n5 y'' =-10y' -15 y\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5y^{″} + 10y^{′} + 15y &= 0 \\\\\ny^{″} + 2y^{′} + 3y &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535418 false] [17592186147315 1145 "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\nm y'' =-cy' -k y .\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k,$ and $c$ gives\n\n$$\n5 y'' =-10y' -15 y .\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{″}+10{y}^{′}+15y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+3y & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535418 true] [17592186147316 1100 "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with light damped harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\ny'' + 2y' + 5y = 0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the damped oscillation?" 13194139535418 true] [17592186147316 1100 "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with light damped harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$y'' + 2y' + 5y = 0$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the damped oscillation?" 13194139535418 false] [17592186147316 1145 "In order to find the period of the damped oscillation, we need to find the solution and then find the period of the sinusoidal terms in the solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we first find the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 + 2\\lambda + 5 = 0\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} + 2λ + 5 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ^{2} + 2λ + 1) - 1 + 5 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 1)^{2} + 4 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 1)^{2} &= -4 \\\\\nλ + 1 &= ± 2\\textrm{i} \\\\\nλ &= -1 ± 2\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny_c =e^{-t}\\left(A\\cos{2t} + B\\sin{2t}\\right)\n$$\n\nFrom the solution, the value of sinusoidal frequency is\n\n$$\n\\Omega = 2\\,\\textrm{s}^{-1}\n$$\n\nThe period of the damped oscillation $T$ is related to the sinusoidal frequency as follows:\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\Omega}\n$$\n\nSo, the period is\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{2} = \\pi \\,\\textrm{s}\n$$" 13194139535418 false] [17592186147316 1145 "In order to find the period of the damped oscillation, we need to find the solution and then find the period of the sinusoidal terms in the solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we first find the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 + 2\\lambda + 5 = 0\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +5 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)-1+5 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-4 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny_c =e^{-t}\\left(A\\cos{2t} + B\\sin{2t}\\right).\n$$\n\nFrom the solution, the value of the angular frequency is $\\Omega = 2\\,\\textrm{s}^{-1}.$\n\nThe period of the damped oscillation $T$ is related to the angular frequency as follows:\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{\\Omega}\n$$\n\nSo, the period is\n\n$$\nT = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{2} = \\pi \\,\\textrm{s}.\n$$" 13194139535418 true] [17592186147383 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348203 13194139535418 true] [17592186147383 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348245 13194139535418 true] [17592186147383 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348266 13194139535418 true] [17592186147383 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348273 13194139535418 true] [17592186147384 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348224 13194139535418 true] [17592186147384 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348231 13194139535418 true] [17592186147384 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348280 13194139535418 true] [17592186147384 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348287 13194139535418 true] [17592186147385 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348210 13194139535418 true] [17592186147385 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348217 13194139535418 true] [17592186147385 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348252 13194139535418 true] [17592186147385 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348294 13194139535418 true] [17592186147386 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348238 13194139535418 true] [17592186147386 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348259 13194139535418 true] [17592186147386 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348301 13194139535418 true] [17592186147386 1020 #edb/ref 17592186348308 13194139535418 true] [17592186148114 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535418 true] [17592186148114 1100 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t$. Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing damped harmonic motion?" 13194139535418 false] [17592186148114 1100 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing damped harmonic motion?" 13194139535418 true] [17592186148114 1145 "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing our equation with the above, we can see that the only equation that represents damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+2y=0.\n$$" 13194139535418 true] [17592186148116 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535418 true] [17592186148116 1100 "A particle of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k=10\\,{\\text{kg/s}}^{2}.$  The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity, and has a damping coefficient $c=4\\,\\text{kg/s}.$  The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t>0$ is the time in seconds.  If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" 13194139535418 true] [17592186148116 1100 "A particle of mass $2kg$ is attached to a massless spring. The spring constant associated with the spring is $k = 10kg/s^{2}$. The particle is also subject to a resistive force that's proportional to the particle's velocity, and has a damping coefficient $c = 4kg/s$. The position of the particle is $y(t)$ (measured in meters), and $t > 0$ is the time in seconds. If the particle moves with damped harmonic motion, what is the governing equation?" 13194139535418 false] [17592186148116 1145 "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\nm{y}^{″}=-c{y}^{′}-ky.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $m,$ $k$ and $c$ gives\n\n$$\n2{y}^{″}=-4{y}^{′}-10y.\n$$\n\nRearranging the above equation, we get:\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+5y=0\n$$" 13194139535418 true] [17592186148117 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535418 true] [17592186148117 1100 "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with light damped harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+10y=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the damped oscillation?" 13194139535418 true] [17592186148117 1100 "The position $y(t)$ of a particle oscillating with light damped harmonic motion is governed by the equation\n\n$y^{″} + 2y^{′} + 10y = 0$,\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, in seconds. What is the period of the damped oscillation?" 13194139535418 false] [17592186148117 1145 "The given equation represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. We are looking for solutions of the form $y={e}^{\\lambda t}.$\n\nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +10=0.\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation can be solved by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +10 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)-1+10 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-9 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}={e}^{-t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡3t+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t).\n$$\n\nFrom the solution, the value of the angular frequency is $Ω=3\\,{\\text{s}}^{-1}.$\n\nThe period of the damped oscillation $T$ is related to the angular frequency as follows:\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{Ω}\n$$\n\nSo the period is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s}.\n$$" 13194139535418 true] [17592186148126 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535418 true] [17592186148126 1100 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping.\n- ${y}^{″}+10{y}^{′}+25y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping.\n- ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+3y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping" 13194139535418 true] [17592186148126 1100 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y^{″} + 2y^{′} + 2y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing light damping.\n2. $y^{″} + 10y^{′} + 25y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical damping.\n3. $y^{″} + 5y^{′} + 3y = 0$ represents an oscillator undergoing heavy damping" 13194139535418 false] [17592186148126 1145 "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+2y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +2=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={2}^{2}-4(1)(2) & =-4<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents light damping, and so statement I is correct.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+10{y}^{′}+25y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +25=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={10}^{2}-4(1)(25) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement II is correct.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+3y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +3=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={5}^{2}-4(1)(3) & =13>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement III is correct.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139535418 true] [17592186348203 1017 #edb/ref 17592186348204 13194139535418 true] [17592186348203 1089 "q-64588" 13194139535418 true] [17592186348203 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535418 true] [17592186348203 1100 "The function $y(t)$ gives the position of a particle at time $t.$ Which of the following equations represents a situation in which the particle is undergoing damped harmonic motion?" 13194139535418 true] [17592186348203 1113 #uuid "18c0275c-179c-553e-bebe-a7b02a771fe7" 13194139535418 true] [17592186348203 1145 "The equation of damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+b{y}^{′}+{\\omega }^{2}y=0.\n$$\n\nComparing our equation with the above, we can see that the only equation that represents damped harmonic motion is\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+12{y}^{′}+6y=0.\n$$" 13194139535418 true] [17592186348204 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348205 13194139535418 t