n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+8y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +8=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={4}^{2}-4(1)(8) & =-16<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents light damping, and so statement I is correct.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={6}^{2}-4(1)(9) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement II is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +2=0\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={3}^{2}-4(1)(2) & =1>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139535418 true] [17592186348225 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348226 13194139535418 true] [17592186348225 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348227 13194139535418 true] [17592186348225 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348228 13194139535418 true] [17592186348225 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348229 13194139535418 true] [17592186348225 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348230 13194139535418 true] [17592186348225 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535418 true] [17592186348225 1041 "selection" 13194139535418 true] [17592186348225 1104 #edb/ref 17592186348228 13194139535418 true] [17592186348225 1110 #uuid "843f15d2-dae7-5145-8dd3-1ca1f3a80ec