going light damping\n- ${y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+4y=0$ represents an oscillator undergoing critical  damping" 13194139535418 true] [17592186348280 1113 #uuid "2c740c4a-060a-582d-bc23-6130ccbffc30" 13194139535418 true] [17592186348280 1145 "Remember that, given the equation of a damped harmonic oscillator, the type of damping depends on the discriminant of the characteristic equation:\n\n- $D>0$ leads to heavy damping,\n\n- $D=0$ leads to critical damping, and\n\n- $D<0$ leads to light damping.\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each equation.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +2=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={3}^{2}-4(1)(2) & =1>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement I is true.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=0$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={6}^{2}-4(1)(9) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents critical damping, and so statement II is false.\n\n- The differential equation ${y}^{″}+7{y}^{′}+4y$ represents the equation of damped harmonic motion. \nThe auxiliary equation for $\\lambda $ is\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +4=0.\n$$\n\nThe discriminant of this quadratic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={7}^{2}-4(1)(4) & =33>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the given equation represents heavy damping, and so statement III is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139535418 true] [17592186348281 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348282 13194139535418 true] [17592186348281 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348283 13194139535418 true] [17592186348281 1000 #edb/ref 17592186348284 13194139535418 true] [175921